          "text": "# Alnair, the Crane\u2019s Blue Needle\n\n*In the southern sky, a bright blue-white star marks a quiet point of reference\u2014and a reminder that even the stars shift.*\n\nAlnair is a small word for a striking sight. From southern latitudes, look toward the constellation Grus, the Crane, and its brightest star stands out against the dark. In the catalogue, Alnair is listed as **alpha Gruis**, with a visual magnitude of 1.71: bright enough to see without binoculars when the sky is clear and dark. Its position, near right ascension 22 hours 08 minutes and declination 47 degrees south, puts it deep in the southern celestial hemisphere. [SIMBAD: Alnair / HIP 109268](https://simbad.cds.unistra.fr/simbad/sim-id?Ident=HIP%20109268)\n\nThere is something pleasing about finding a star by its place in a pattern. Grus is not as famous as the Southern Cross, but its stars trace a long-necked bird in flight. Alnair is the brightest point in that figure, a steady marker for the eye. Let your gaze adjust to the dark, then scan the southern sky rather than looking directly at the star at first; peripheral vision can make faint surrounding stars easier to notice. Binoculars will show more of the field, though Alnair itself needs no optical aid.\n\nThe catalogue classifies Alnair as B6V. The \u201cB\u201d places it among hot, blue-white stars; \u201cV\u201d denotes a main-sequence star, in the broad stellar classification system. It is not a nearby lamp in the foreground of the Milky Way: its measured parallax is 32.29 milliarcseconds. Taking the inverse of that value gives a distance of about 31 parsecs, or roughly 101 light-years. The light reaching your eyes tonight began its journey when events on Earth were very different.\n\nThat distance is inferred from parallax\u2014the tiny apparent shift of a nearby star against more distant background stars as Earth travels around the Sun. The shift is minute, but carefully measured. Alnair\u2019s catalogue entry also records its proper motion: the star\u2019s apparent drift across the sky over time. The celestial patterns seem fixed during a human evening, yet they are not frozen. With precise observations separated by years, their changing positions become measurable.\n\nFor a useful observing moment, aim to see Alnair when it is high above the horizon, where its light passes through less of Earth\u2019s turbulent atmosphere. Its right ascension means it crosses the local meridian when the local sidereal time is about 22 hours; as a rough seasonal guide, that puts it high in the evening sky during southern spring. The exact hour depends on your location and date. From far-northern locations, the star stays below the horizon; even farther south, it climbs higher and becomes an easier target.\n\nThe best equipment is a dark patch of sky, a little patience, and a clear southern horizon. Find the Crane, pick out its brightest star, and hold in mind both scales at once: a point of light in a familiar pattern, and a hot star roughly a hundred light-years away, slowly changing its place in the sky.\n\n**Source:** [SIMBAD astronomical database entry for HIP 109268 (Alnair)](https://simbad.cds.unistra.fr/simbad/sim-id?Ident=HIP%20109268)."
